And yet, might not a big part of the slow
development issue be that developing in C, pretty
close now to a backward (but not yet dead) language,
compared to OOPLs, makes investing coding experience
into a language not otherwise wonderfully useful to
the participants, look like time down a rat-hole
compared to investing the same time on a project in
a language that leads to high employability?

I could see developers getting really burned out on
working in a language in "effectively dead" status
per employment prospects. When Rogue started the
family of Roguelike languages, C was what, 8 years
old? That made it a new kid on the block. Now C is
36 years old, and much better programming languages
have come along since it was born.

